  "content": {
    "title": "What to Know About JFK Jr. and Carolyn Bessette's Burial at Sea",
    "text": "John F. Kennedy Jr. and Carolyn Bessette in May 1999.Credit: Justin Ide/NewsmakersNEED TO KNOWJohn F. Kennedy Jr., Carolyn Bessette and her sister Lauren Bessette died on July 16, 1999Following their deaths, the political heir and the Bessette sisters were crematedThe families gathered on July 22, 1999, to scatter their ashes at seaOn July 22, 1999, the families of John F. Kennedy Jr., Carolyn Bessette and her sister Lauren Bessette joined together for their burial at sea.The George co-founder and the Bessette sisters died on July 16, 1999, when a small plane piloted by John plunged into the Atlantic Ocean off the coast of Martha’s Vineyard. On July 21, 1999, Navy divers recovered their bodies from the ocean floor. One year later, the National Transportation Safety Board declared in a report that pilot error was the most likely cause of the plane crash.John and Carolyn were memorialized at a private funeral at St. Thomas Church in New York in July 1999. The service was attended by members of their families, as well as then-President Bill Clinton and John's \"hero,\" Muhammad Ali, per PEOPLE editor-at-large Liz McNeil and JFK Jr.'s former assistant RoseMarie Terenzio's 2024 book, JFK Jr.: An Intimate Oral Biography.Prior to the funeral, members of their families gathered for a burial at sea on July 22, 1999, when John, Carolyn and Lauren's ashes were scattered into the ocean.Here's everything to know about JFK Jr., Carolyn Bessette and Lauren Bessette's burial at sea.There were disagreements about whether to bury or cremate themJohn F. Kennedy Jr. and Carolyn Bessette in March 1999.Credit: Arnaldo Magnani/LiaisonAccording to McNeil and Terenzio's book, there were some disagreements between the families about whether to bury or cremate the couple.Initially, Ted Kennedy wanted his nephew to be buried in the family plot in Brookline, Mass. However, Lauren couldn't be buried there since she wasn't a Kennedy family member, and the sisters' mother Ann Freeman did not want her daughters separated, per McNeil and Terenzio's book.There was then a meeting about the funeral with the Bessette family. Caroline Kennedy's husband Ed Schlossberg and Ted's wife Victoria \"Vicki\" Reggie went to represent the Kennedy family.\"Vicki was trying to convince them to bury John and Carolyn in Brookline,\" Terenzio shared in the oral biography, adding that she was \"inserting herself\" in the situation, which she believes the Bessette family found \"disrespectful.\"The families eventually decided to spread the ashesCarolyn Bessette, John F. Kennedy Jr. and Caroline Kennedy on Oct. 4, 1998, in New York.Credit: Sonia Moskowitz/GettyOnce cremation was considered, Terenzio shared in the book that John's sister Caroline called and asked \"how Catholic\" he was, in an effort to be sure spreading the ashes would \"be the right thing.\"\"I said, 'Caroline, I think you should cremate them, and they should all stay together. I think John would want that,' \" Terenzio recalled in the book. \"She wanted to honor her brother.\"Following the meeting between the families, they decided on a burial at sea.Their ashes were spread into the waters off Martha's Vineyard in late July 1999Kennedy and Bessette family members aboard the coast guard cutter YSS Briscoe for the burial of John F. Kennedy Jr., Carolyn Bessette and Lauren Bessette.Credit: Najlah Feanny/Corbis via GettyJFK Jr., Carolyn and Lauren's ashes were spread into the waters off Martha's Vineyard on July 22, 1999. According to CNN, they were spread in the vicinity of the plane crash, which occurred about seven miles off the Massachusetts island.The burial at sea took place on a U.S. Navy destroyer, the USS Briscoe, and only 17 members of the Kennedy, Bessette and Freeman families came aboard, Navy chaplain William Petruska told McNeil and Terenzio.Navy chaplain Louis Iasiello told McNeil and Terenzio that the \"only controversial thing\" was that there was debate over whether anyone was entitled to a burial at sea from a naval vessel. However, President Bill Clinton \"quickly put an end to that discussion,\" Iasiello said. (Pentagon officials told CNN that there are two grounds for granting permission for a naval burial at sea, one of which allows it for the children of decorated Navy veterans. John's father, former President John F. Kennedy, served in World War II.)Reflecting on the day, Iasiello said he vividly remembers \"the way that Senator Kennedy went from family member to family member, a fatherly figure ministering to the family. His way of touching them, giving them reassurance that they were all together as a family and as friends.\"Iasiello also recalled \"moving\" hymns being sung. Navy chaplain Barry C. Black, who carried the urns with John and Carolyn's ashes, said that despite having officiated over 100 funerals, he had \"not seen such palpable grief anywhere in my life.\"Black also recalled a moment he shared with Freeman. He told her he had lost his mother, and shared his \"anger at God and the fact that God is big enough to handle our rage.\"Freeman hadn't responded at the time, but later returned to Black and whispered to him, \"What you said back there is the first thing that has made any sense since this whole horrible ordeal,\" Black recalled in the oral biography.In remembrance, several mourners scattered flowers, and others also threw envelopes with notes inside, into the sea below.Carolyn and Lauren's parents took \"solace\" in knowing that John and Carolyn will be with Lauren \"for eternity\"Carolyn and Lauren Bessette in 1982.Credit: CourtesyIn July 1999, the Bessette family shared a statement thanking those who assisted in the exhaustive search for their children, while also acknowledging the decision for Lauren, Carolyn and John's ashes to be scattered together.\"John F. Kennedy Jr and Carolyn Bessette were true soul mates, and we hope to honor them in death in the simple manner in which they chose to live their lives. We take solace in the thought that together they will comfort Lauren for eternity,\" the statement read, according to The New York Times.John's father previously spoke about the meaning of the seaJohn F. Kennedy Jr. at The Onassis Beach House in Greece on July 21, 1970.Credit: Ron Galella/Ron Galella Collection via GettyJohn's father, President Kennedy, spoke about the sea's greater meaning during a speech at the Australian Ambassador’s Dinner for the America’s Cup crews in September 1962.\"I really don’t know why it is that all of us are so committed to the sea, except I think it’s because in addition to the fact that the sea changes, and the light changes, and ships change, it’s because we all came from the sea,\" he said. \"And it is an interesting biological fact that all of us have, in our veins, the exact same percentage of salt in our blood that exists in the ocean, and, therefore, we have salt in our blood, in our sweat, in our tears.\"President Kennedy continued, \"We are tied to the ocean. And when we go back to the sea—whether it is to sail or to watch it—we are going back from whence we came.\"Read the original article on People",
